t bytes\" conflict never goes through the generated product. It runs only as one fixed scenario at the end of each case, where the peer holds nothing but the conflicting copy.\n- **The plant that survived:** I narrowed `DurableIndex.SyncFrom` so it checks the bytes of shared identities only when every identity the peer holds is also local. It passed both the configured suite and product shard N3.\n- **Why it matters:** with that change, a peer that sends a conflicting copy along with any new object leaves the two replicas silently holding different bytes, which is exactly what the acceptance criteria forbid.\n- **Reproduction:** a small test where the peer has the conflicting lease plus one extra object. It passes on the candidate and fails with the plant applied. Raw logs are in the evidence tar.\n\n**Everything else I reran myself on the exact tree `c7549f1`:**\n\n| Check | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Registry, decoded | Held. Every new acceptance case appears in its clause lists (§11.4, §5.3, §10.7). No trunk row was lost or changed. |\n| Registry digest | Matches the pinned value. Removing one case from clause 11.4#7 turns tracecheck red. |\n| tracecheck | Green, 88/610 clauses discharged. |\n| README coverage figure | Changing it at either site (the fenced report line or the prose sentence) makes the tracecheck tests fail. |\n| Full `go test ./...` | 45 of 46 packages ok in my copy. `specpin` failed only because I left `.task-board` out of the copy; it passes in the live worktree. |\n| `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` and gofmt | Clean. |\n| Importer comparison, base `6d3bff9` vs candidate (24 packages) | 0 test results changed or removed. |\n| Determinism | `TestDurableSync*` passed with `-count=3`; product shards N1–4 and N2 passed. |\n| `task-board.config.json` | Byte-identical to the base. |\n\n**Own plants:** four of the five narrowings I planted (timestamp tie-break, partial peer regressing a local root, non-idempotent re-sync, accepting a whitespace-only byte difference) each failed the tests when run alone. The neutral control survived, so the harness can report a survivor. The fifth plant is the one above.\n\n**Not done / non-blocking:**\n- I did not rerun the producer's 77-row mutation harness (time budget). My five plants and the control stand in for it.\n- The 38,000-case product skips itself under plain `go test ./...`, so the configured validation suite never runs it. Every plant I killed also died in the smaller named tests, so this isn't blocking yet. I recommended adding a small always-on shard.\n- The case that expects the tombstone conflict names its position in the object list directly, so it breaks if that list is reordered.\n\nThe verdict (`TASK-260830-2h5uv9_review-verdict-rev3.md`) and evidence tar are attached and read back byte-identical. The verdict includes the findings array and a rework scope for the producer. I checked the two reviewer checklist items, then deleted the scratch directory; the live worktree is unchanged. I also saved a memory note about conflict branches that sit outside a generated product.","ttft_ms":2172,"type":"result","duration_ms":2030577,"uuid":"0734357c-8b8c-43db-a877-c65a149c0d1b","ttft_stream_ms":1293,"time_to_request_ms":35,"first_content_frame_ms":1293,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
